A||||7||billk||exhaust noise||||1||
Z||000000||billk||03-08-2001||12:10 PM||||I have a 2.8 1988.  I think the exhaust which is the OEM type is too loud.  Are there ways of making my car quieter, particularly at 50-60 mph?||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||03-08-2001||12:42 PM||||A good stereo system helps.<P>Seriously, you may want to check your system for holes. If the car has the original exhaust that spent 13 yers on the east coast, I wouldn't be shocked to find rust-through. Even with my Flowmaster and big pipes the exhaust note is rather pleasing and non-intrusive.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||03-08-2001||12:42 PM||||You sure its not leaking?  You might be able to buy some aftermarket anti-resonator.  My brother has a Camry with 2 of them, one went bad and it got a lot louder.  Of course Fiero's don't have them but you might be able to add one where the crossover pipe goes down to the cat. <P>I would check for an exhaust leak first though.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fieropage/af.bmp"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||03-08-2001||12:42 PM||||oops<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 03-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||c m west||03-08-2001||12:51 PM||||The two most probable causes are either a worn out exhaust " donut " between the y pipe and the catalytic pipe connection - second cause would be a broken weold on either of the two headers<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero] <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||billk||03-08-2001||02:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>A good stereo system helps.<P>Seriously, you may want to check your system for holes. If the car has the original exhaust that spent 13 yers on the east coast, I wouldn't be shocked to find rust-through. Even with my Flowmaster and big pipes the exhaust note is rather pleasing and non-intrusive.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>There is apparently no hole in the exhaust.  I thought maybe putting resonator tips on the exhaust pipes might help, but a muffler guy does not think so.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||03-08-2001||08:23 PM||||Does the exhaust seem louder inside the car than outside?  If so, maybe the exhaust is contacting the frame or body.  Check exhaust hangers.  My car ('85 SE 2.5) also has an L-shaped bracket (buffer?) that clamps to the exhaust; this bracket can contact the cradle.<P>Also, does your car still have a cataclysmic perverter?  If the cat has been removed, you car <I>will</I> be louder.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 03-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||03-09-2001||02:40 PM||||Stock Fieros are pretty quiet all things considered...<P>West is right though. Check to make sure that the cat is blown out. Empty cats can make you loud without showing any holes. <P>Check the front exhaust manifold (yeah the one you can't really get too...) and check for cracks. In the past we had manifolds that were cracked all the way around and were very LOUD! When we took the manifold out they litterally came out in pieces.<P>Hope this helps.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 03-09-2001).]||||reg||3
